{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/nginx","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:10.805Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/cbl-mariner/base/nginx/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/cbl-mariner/base/nginx/about"},"readme":"***Warning: Azure Linux 2.0 will reach its End of Life (EOL) on July 31st, 2025. After this date, it will no longer receive updates, security patches, or support, which could leave your systems vulnerable. We strongly recommend upgrading to an Azure Linux 3.0 image before July 31st, 2025.***\n\n## About\n\nThis repository contains the official Nginx image for CBL-Mariner.\n\n### What is CBL-Mariner?\n\nCBL-Mariner is an internal Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. CBL-Mariner is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about CBL-Mariner, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is Nginx?\n\nNginx (pronounced \"engine-x\") is an open-source reverse proxy server for HTTP, HTTPS, SMTP, POP3, and IMAP protocols, as well as a load balancer, HTTP cache, and a web server (origin server). The nginx project started with a strong focus on high concurrency, high performance, and low memory usage. It is licensed under the 2-clause BSD-like license and it runs on Linux, BSD variants, Mac OS X, Solaris, AIX, HP-UX, as well as on other *nix flavors. It also has a proof-of-concept port for Microsoft Windows.\nSee: [Nginx - Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nginx)\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `1`\n- `1-cm2.0`\n\nThe featured tags are multi-architecture tags (i.e., `x86-64` and `aarch64`) that refer to the latest version of the image. Additional tags that are specific to nginx version, Mariner build, or architecture can be found in the Tags section.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Nginx configuration folder\n\nThe default location for the nginx configuration folder is: `/etc/nginx/`. The nginx's primary configuration file is `nginx.conf`. It has an include directive at the end of the file i.e., `include /etc/nginx/nginx.conf.default;`. If you'd like to provide your nginx default file, then consider naming it `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf.default`.\n\n### Hosting some simple static content\n\n`1.` Create a Dockerfile to generate a new image that includes the necessary content.\n\n    FROM mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/nginx:1\n    COPY static-html-directory /usr/share/nginx/html\n\n`2.` Place this file in the same directory as your directory of content (“static-html-directory”). Then build your container image.\n\n    docker build -t static-html-nginx .\n\n`3.` Start your container.\n\n    docker run -d -p 8080:80 static-html-nginx\n\nThe default container startup command is `\"nginx\", \"-g\", \"daemon off;\"`.\n\n### Launch Mariner shell\n\nThe nginx image ships with a bash shell. The launch command can be provided to docker run command to launch Mariner shell. The following command will launch the shell:\n\n    docker run -it mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/nginx:1 /bin/bash\n\n## Support\n\nCBL-Mariner container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\nThe CBL-Mariner distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslegalnotice)","readmeBase":null,"pub